{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach: (1) Broad survey of raw feature families across all lookbacks to map factor axes and signs - neg() only flips SR sign so one direction per family suffices, halving budget. Found medium-term momentum (peak 60d, SR 1.52), short-term reversal (3-5d), a positive kurtosis premium (positive at EVERY lookback 5 to 250, so the axis is real not a lookback artifact), and weak low-beta / low-correlation / low-vol effects. (2) Confirmed cs_rank and cs_z are identity under the harness rank-weighting, which makes them ideal scale-free normalizers for blending heterogeneous features via add/sub; mul of two centered ranks flips sign and is useless. (3) Momentum improves markedly when the recent week is removed - both sub(ret(60), ret(5)) and the cleaner delay(ret(60), 5) - consistent with short-term reversal contaminating raw 60d momentum. Volatility scaling by std(20) adds further. (4) The big gain is blending risk-adjusted skip-momentum with kurtosis in rank space: the two legs are near-orthogonal, roughly 1.99 and 1.93 standalone combining to 2.65. Kurtosis peaks at the 15d window. (5) Remaining work: neighbourhood robustness checks around the winner and leg-weighting, then pick three signals that are structurally distinct rather than three near-duplicates of the single best fit, to limit train-window overfitting.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:24:16.055270+00:00"}
