{"kind": "journal", "text": "Selection logic. The search converged on one economic structure: volatility-scaled skip-momentum (60d return, most recent week removed via delay, divided by a short-window realized vol) blended 50/50 in rank space with a tail leg (high kurtosis, negative skew). Standalone the legs run about 2.0-2.1 SR each and combine to roughly 2.9-3.0, so they are close to orthogonal - the classic cross-sectional momentum premium plus a tail-shape premium. Robustness evidence: the peak is a broad smooth plateau, not a knife edge. Momentum window 40/60/120, delay 3/5/10/15, vol denominator 5/10/15/20/60, kurtosis 10/15/20/40/60 and skew 5/20/40/60 all stay in the 2.2-3.0 band, and ret vs logret is interchangeable. The kurtosis premium is positive at every window from 5 to 250, so the axis is genuine even though 15d is the best single choice. Adding third legs (reversal, low beta, low correlation, low vol, maxr) consistently hurt once momentum and tails were both in. Final three were chosen from the balanced 50/50 ensemble family rather than the marginally higher train-SR family that overweights the tail leg 2 to 1. Rationale: the two families differ by about 0.03-0.07 train SR, which is well inside the standard error of a Sharpe estimate, but the balanced family carries systematically higher rank IC (0.0150-0.0154 vs 0.0136), indicating a broader cross-sectional edge that is less dependent on a few high-leverage days. Overweighting kurtosis is also the specific risk I least want to take out of sample, since the kurtosis signal decays fast (delaying it 5 days halves its SR) and its 15d peak is the one lookback choice most likely to be fitted. The three reported expressions average over one, two and three momentum specifications respectively against a two-specification tail leg, so lookback-selection noise is diversified across the set.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:29:20.249423+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["add(cs_rank(add(cs_rank(add(cs_rank(div(delay(ret(60), 5), std(5))), cs_rank(div(delay(ret(60), 10), std(20))))), cs_rank(div(sub(ret(60), ret(5)), std(5))))), cs_rank(add(cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(40))), cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(5))))))", "add(add(cs_rank(div(delay(ret(60), 5), std(5))), cs_rank(div(delay(ret(60), 10), std(20)))), add(cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(40))), cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(5)))))", "add(cs_rank(div(delay(ret(60), 5), std(5))), cs_rank(add(cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(40))), cs_rank(sub(kurt(15), skew(5))))))"], "chosen_sr_train": [2.9422, 2.908, 2.9756], "rationale": "All three implement the same economic structure: volatility-scaled skip-momentum blended 50/50 in rank space with a tail-shape leg (high kurtosis, negative skew). The legs are near-orthogonal, running about 2.0-2.1 train SR standalone and combining to 2.91-2.98. The optimum is a broad smooth plateau across every lookback axis (momentum 40-120, delay 3-15, vol denominator 5-60, kurtosis 10-60, skew 5-60 all sit in the 2.2-3.0 band) and the kurtosis premium is positive at every window from 5 to 250, so the structure is not a lookback artifact. I chose the balanced 50/50 family over a variant scoring 3.005 that overweights the tail leg 2 to 1: the train SR gap is inside the standard error of a Sharpe estimate, while the balanced family carries systematically higher rank IC (0.0150-0.0154 vs 0.0136), a broader cross-sectional edge less reliant on a few high-leverage days. The three average over one, two and three momentum specifications against a two-specification tail leg, diversifying lookback-selection risk across the reported set.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:29:29.495783+00:00"}
